            "text": "At dawn, a city briefly belongs to the people who notice small things. Delivery trucks sigh at curbs, pigeons negotiate over crumbs, and windows brighten one by one like cautious stars. Before traffic gathers its full voice, the streets reveal a softer machinery: brooms brushing pavement, keys turning in locks, kettles beginning to murmur behind apartment walls.\n\nI like this hour because it makes ordinary life feel freshly invented. A baker lifts a metal shutter, and the smell of bread escapes before the customers arrive. A nurse walks home with tired shoulders. Somewhere, a child wakes too early and asks a question no adult is prepared to answer. None of these moments will become news, yet together they form the hidden foundation of the day.\n\nPerhaps attention is a kind of gratitude. To observe something closely is to admit that it exists beyond our use for it. The tree beside a bus stop is not merely shade; it is bark, sap, weather, nests, scars, and seasons held in one patient body. The stranger across the aisle is not scenery; they are carrying memories, errands, fears, jokes, and private hopes.\n\nBy noon, haste usually wins. Phones ring, engines complain, doors slam, and everyone moves as if pursued by an invisible clock. Still, the morning remains available in memory, a quiet instruction: look again. The world is never empty or dull; our attention is simply elsewhere. Bring it back, and even a familiar street can open like a book beneath your hands."
